  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe low that was going to bring some rain to much of the region\nlater today has struggled to develop early this morning. Models\nhave picked up on this as well with a smaller and weaker low \npassing east of New York State and into the Gulf of Maine. The \nprecipitation shield is also much smaller and hugs the Atlantic \ncoast more. Chances of precipitation were greatly reduced across\nthe whole area with only the Catskills and Poconos holding on \nto some chance PoPs. The low will still be successful in \nscouring out most of the moisture behind it so as a cold front \nmoves in Monday, only a few light rain showers are expected \nalong it. 850 mb temperatures still get between -7c and -9c \nMonday night into Tuesday so lake effect precipitation has been \nkept in from previous forecast. Temperatures will be cool enough\nfor snow for the Finger Lakes into CNY with the lake effect \nthough organized bands is not expected with accumulations \nlargely being a dusting here and there. Temperatures moderate \nquickly Wednesday as a ridge builds in to the northeast as a \ntrough digs into the Central US helping set the stage for a \nstronger area of low pressure later in the week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nModels and their ensemble members have really come into much \nbetter agreement with the late week storm over the last 24 \nhours. The cluster analysis yesterday hinted that the trend was \ntowards a warmer, less amplified trough in the Central US with a\nmore inland track of the low. Most of the deterministic \nguidance does have the inland low and most of the ensemble low \npressure centers are also now over land. The trough digging into\nthe central US does look like it will try and become somewhat \nnegatively tilted so even though the low is taking the western, \nmore inland track, it still looks like it could deepen quite a \nbit. Warm air wraps into the low from the south and east, with \nthe dominant precipitation type being almost all rain. The GEFS \nensemble is the most bullish on snow still with the EPS and \nAIFS-ENS both trending towards less snow with tonight's runs. \nThe GEFS members that bring snowfall to the region all have \nprecipitation types starting as snow and transitioning to rain \nand assumes that the cold air arriving Monday into Tuesday does \nnot warm up much. Most model guidance does have the airmass \nmoderating mid week so the snowier solutions are unlikely at \nthis time. \n\nOne thing to watch may be the winds as the storm deepens. \nModels are hinting at a strong warm conveyer into the low on the\nnorth side with 850 mb winds over 50 knots. Depending on \nstability, there could be some stronger winds to watch out for \nThursday.",
